{"name":"Old Sterling","hex":"#cfd5d1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"old","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Mochi."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of an object, concept, relationship, etc., having existed for a relatively long period of time.","Of a living being, having lived for most of the expected years."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/old"},{"term":"sterling","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An English silver penny first introduced by the Normans.","A penny issued in other countries, such as Scotland.\n The currency of the United Kingdom, also called the pound sterling\n\n Short for sterling silver (“an alloy containing not less than 92.5 percent silver, the remainder usually being copper; articles made from this alloy collectively”).\n\n Former British gold or silver coinage of a standard fineness (0.91666 for gold and 0.925 for silver).\n\n\n Synonym of pennyweight (“a unit of mass equal to 24 grains, or ⁄20 of a troy ounce”)\n\n Money generally.\n The standard degree of fineness."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to the currency of the United Kingdom, or former types of English or British coinage.\n\nOf silver: being of standard quality.\nOf a thing: of or relating to, or made from, sterling silver.\n\n Of English (or  Scottish) currency: genuine, of legal tender.","Of or relating to the currency of the United Kingdom, or former types of English or British coinage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sterling"}],"entries":[],"images":[],"sounds":{"old":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/En-uk-old.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/4/4f/En-uk-old.ogg/En-uk-old.ogg.mp3"},{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia